                <head>201. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>Representative to the United Nations Arthur J.
                        Goldberg met with President Johnson at the
                    LBJ Ranch on November 7, 1966. A November 9 memorandum from Nathaniel Davis of
                    the NSC staff to Special Assistant Walt
                        Rostow reports that Chinese representation was among
                    the subjects Goldberg discussed with the
                    President. (Johnson Library, National Security File, Agency File, United
                    Nations, Vol. 5) A statement by Goldberg,
                    released to the press by the White House Press Secretary’s office in San
                    Antonio, Texas, on November 7, included the statement that the United States did
                    not wish to increase the isolation of mainland China from the rest of the world
                    but that it would not consent to “the demands of Peking that the Republic of
                    China on Taiwan be excluded and the UN itself
                    transformed, in order to pave the way for Peking’s admission.” For the text, see
                    Department of State Bulletin, December 5, 1966, pages 851–855.</p>
            </div>
